# Intro
A star lights the gas at rest inside a sphere around it, then a molecular cloud that
starts at a spherical face centred on the star (`0.25 L` from it) and moves radially away
from it. Light that crosses gas moving by more than three line widths
(`3 b`, `b = sqrt(2 k T / m_H2)`, 0.64 km/s at 50 K) relative to the column it carries sees
fresh absorption: the transport resets the carried column. The check reads particles
inside the cloud and compares their line transmission with the bar of the reset (the
column of the cloud alone) or of no reset (the column of all the gas from the star). Read
the README of `ISRFH2StarLit` for the build, the table, the parameters that the run holds
fixed and the exit classes.

# Run
```
./run.sh                       # cloud at 10 widths: expect the reset
widths=1 run_name=control ./run.sh   # cloud at 1 width: expect no reset
```
`makeIC.py --widths` sets the radial speed of the cloud in units of `b`. The check picks
the expected case from the speed written in `fixture.json`: more than 3 widths gives the
reset.

# Checking
Per particle in the cloud at `r < L/2 - 2h`, with `d` the path from the cloud face to the
particle and `R = gamma h_max` the largest hop of the transport (`gamma = 1.936` for the
Wendland C2 kernel), the own ratio lies between `f(n_max (d + R))` and `f(n_min d)` for the
reset, and between `f(n_max r)` and `f(n_min r)` for no reset, with `f` the shielding
function and `n_min`, `n_max` the extreme H2 densities of the gas in the snapshot. The
bar is one-sided in the reset case because the hop that resets also books the part of its
path in front of the cloud, between zero and one `R`. The speed of 1 width is the
control: a run that shows a reset there fails.
